# The input grammar (IG-1…IG-15)

**Ruled by Rick, 2026-10-13, as Round 0 Part A of the input-grammar
round.** One grammar, project-wide, designed first: every binary is built
to it; no surface is the reference, and nothing is patched around an
existing spelling. There is no backward compatibility before genesis: a
renamed spelling leaves no trace and no migration table; our own scripts
and configs are fixed in the same change. The glossary tree and the name
tables are in [`INPUT_GRAMMAR.md`](../../docs/design/INPUT_GRAMMAR.md);
this rule holds the rules only.

## Scope

Every identifier a person types or a script depends on: command-line
flags of every shipped binary, config-file keys, command words of
`shekyl-cli` (and of the daemon console until it moves into
`shekyl-cli`), enumeration keywords, unit spellings, and REJECTED names.
Out of scope: RPC method names (the RPC lane's wire contract) and log
category names (`shekyl-logging`'s).

## The rules

**IG-1 Lexical.** An identifier is lowercase ASCII, `[a-z][a-z0-9]*`
segments joined by single hyphens. Identifiers, keywords and units are
never translated.

**IG-2 One vocabulary, as a tree.** The glossary is a tree of terms. Each
term is defined once, project-wide, and lists its parents; a term with no
parent is a *subject*. A term reused under several parents keeps its one
meaning. Every segment of every name is a term in the tree, including
plain words (`file`, `dir`, `max`, `help`): the gate has no exemption
list.

**IG-3 Structure.** A name is a path through the tree: it starts at a
subject and each segment is a child of the one before. A subject alone is
a name. The qualifiers `max` and `min` may end any path. A path-valued
option ends in `file` or `dir`. A term may both carry a value and have
children. `dev` is a subject like any other (IG-8); its children may be
other subjects. The gate checks that a name is well formed; whether it is
an option is the table's. A command is a path whose last term is a
*verb* (the command acts) or a *view* (a noun whose command prints it);
verbs and views are terms of that kind under their parent, a subject that
is itself a verb or a view is a command alone (`send`, `balance`), and
each command has exactly one spelling. A view changes no state; a
command that changes state ends in a verb. The gate walks every live and
reserved name and every command in every binary's table (a REJECTED row is a
refused spelling, exempt by kind; IG-10) and fails on: an undefined
segment; an adjacent pair that is not a parent→child edge; a qualifier
anywhere but last; a term defined twice. The edit that proves it can
fail: add an option `socks-tor`.

**IG-4 No negation.** No term is `no`, `non`, `not`, `disable`, `enable`,
`ignore`, `skip` or `without`. A switch defaults to off. A capability
that is on by default is a closed set (`<on|off>`, or named modes).

**IG-5 One option per value; one name per option.** No two options set
the same value; no aliases; no hidden duplicates; no short flags; no
prefix abbreviation. A rename leaves no trace.

**IG-6 Value kinds.** A kind is a syntax, and every option has exactly
one: switch; one-of (a closed lowercase keyword set, default named);
integer (decimal, no sign unless the row allows it, no separators);
quantity (an integer and a unit — bytes `B KiB MiB GiB TiB`, IEC only,
`KB`/`MB` refused; rates the same over `/s`; durations `ms s min h d`);
endpoint (a literal IP and a port; no hostnames); peer-endpoint (an
endpoint or `<v3>.onion:port`); address (a Shekyl address, checked
against the network); origin (an explicit web origin, RFC 6454: scheme `http` or
`https`, a host, an optional port, nothing after; `*` is refused); hash
(64 hexadecimal characters, a 32-byte value: a transaction id, a block
hash or a key image); amount (a
decimal SKL amount with at most `DISPLAY_DECIMAL_POINT` fractional
digits, `shekyl-units` the one source, parsed exactly to atomic units,
never a float); name (a wallet's name: one path component, no
separators); text (the rest of the line, verbatim); path
(absolute only); secret (file only; never logged or echoed, shown in the
startup line only as set; zeroized; held only in Rust); command (file only; an
argument array run without a shell; placeholders are `{name}`, each
name a glossary term under the row with one meaning everywhere, braces
the only syntax, `%` literal, an unknown name refused); list
and map of a scalar kind (the flag repeated; `none` is the empty list or map; a
command-line list or map replaces the file's whole). A row narrows its
kind with a constraint enforced by its constructor and may admit named
keywords (`unlimited`, `auto`, `stdin`; paths are absolute, so a keyword
never collides with a file). A row with no default is *unset* when not
given, and the row states what unset means; there are no sentinel
values. There are no floating-point values anywhere in the grammar.

**IG-7 Surfaces.** Each row says where it may be given. Command-line
only: `help`, `version`, `network`, `config-file`, `service` (each selects
which file is read, so a key in that file could only restate or
contradict it). File only: every secret and every command value.
Everything else: both, CLI winning per key.

**IG-8 Development levers.** A `dev-` option is hidden from help and
generated docs, names the networks it is accepted on as data in its row
(a `dev-` option given on any other network is refused at parse, rule
71), and is announced at every start when set. It follows every other
rule. No `dev-` option is ever accepted on mainnet: a lever mainnet
genuinely needs is not a development lever and gets a public row.

**IG-9 One table per binary, generated everything.** The table row: id,
name, kind, constraint, keywords, default (a value, unset, or "derived
from the network row"), surfaces, runtime (`yes` or `no`: whether
`option set` accepts the row, IG-12), grant (`health`, `status`, `peers`
or `config`, default `config`: the grant under which `option show`
discloses the row; RPC_CHANNEL §6.4, RT-O24), visibility (`public` /
`dev` / `rejected` / `reserved`), accepted networks (`dev` only), owning
subsystem, help text. A row names `health` only if it passes
RPC_CHANNEL §6.1's line for that grant: a client needs it to decide
whether it can use the node now, and it says nothing lasting about the
node. The peer, closed-peer and ban rows are `peers`; everything that
describes the host or links the operator (`mine-address`, `tor-onion`,
paths, SOCKS endpoints, notify commands) is `config`; a secret's value is
shown under no grant. From the table: `--help`, completion, the
documentation tables, and an annotated TOML example. A gate regenerates
the docs and diffs them, and asserts the table is non-empty (rule 47).

**IG-10 Unknown and refused names.** An unknown name is one general
refusal that names the nearest live name, computed from the table (edit
distance), never from a hand list. A REJECTED row (a feature deliberately
refused) carries its reason and a pointer to the decision; it is never
accepted, never in help, and its refusal states the reason. A REJECTED
row is a contract entry in the sense of rule 23, not a code symbol: it is
never deleted as dead. A RESERVED row (a name held for a ruled future
option; rule 23's reserved slot) is the same kind of entry: never
accepted, never in help, and its refusal names what the name is reserved
for and where that is ruled.

**IG-11 Diagnostics.** Every refusal is a typed value: a stable code
(`config.value.range`, `config.file.permissions`, …), the option, the
source (`cli`, `file:path:line`, `default`, `runtime`) and named parameters. One
renderer turns it into the English message, which states what was wrong,
where, and what to do (rule 82). Machine output and logs carry the code.
A configuration refusal exits with status 2; a runtime failure with 1.

**IG-12 Runtime changes share the constructor.** A value that can also
change while running (rates, log level and categories, mining) is marked
runtime in its row (IG-9) and is built by the same constructor on both
surfaces, with the same refusals. `option set <name> <value>` changes it:
it refuses a row not marked runtime with a typed code, requires the
`node` grant, and never writes the file. `option show [<name>]` reports
each value with its source (`default`, `file`, `cli`, `runtime`) and
returns each row whose grant (IG-9) the caller holds; a row the caller
may not see is absent, never emptied, and a secret's value is shown under
no grant, only whether it is set. Every runtime row's default is writable
as a value (`unlimited`, `none`), so `option set` returns a row to its
default without a restart; what `option show` prints is valid input to
`option set` and builds the same value; and no row is both runtime and a
secret, which the gate asserts (RPC_CHANNEL §6.4, RT-O25, RT-O27). Mining
changes while running through `mine start` and `mine stop` instead,
which build `mine-address` and `mine-threads` through their
constructors, so those rows are not marked runtime. The live value is
owned by its subsystem. No binary ever writes its own config file.

**IG-13 Inputs come from the table, and nowhere else.** No option value
comes from the environment. Default paths come from the operating
system's account and known-folder interfaces, never from `HOME`, `XDG_*`
or `%APPDATA%`. Each binary's per-account root is the passwd entry's home
plus `.shekyl` on Linux, FreeBSD and macOS, and
`SHGetKnownFolderPath(FOLDERID_LocalAppData)` plus `Shekyl` on Windows,
never Roaming AppData, which copies the wallet and its config to domain
servers (rule 36); a run-as-user daemon's data and log defaults, the wallet
directory and the wallet binaries' config files sit under it. On Windows
the daemon's run mode selects its root, and every default path it derives
(`config-file`, `data-dir`, `log-file`) sits under that root: run-as-user
uses the per-account root and `service` uses
`%ProgramData%\shekyl\`, which only the installer creates (owner and ACL:
Administrators, SYSTEM and the service account); the daemon never creates
the machine-wide root, and one that is missing, mis-owned or writable by
others refuses with a typed code. On Linux, FreeBSD and macOS the daemon
has one run mode (`service` is refused there): its config file is
`/etc/shekyl/`, and its data and log defaults sit under the per-account
root. The only environment reads permitted are presentation-only
(terminal capabilities that change rendering, never a value). Every
resolved default path appears in the startup line with its source. A
service account whose passwd home is unset or `/nonexistent` has no
default paths: the packaging sets the account's home or the unit passes
`--data-dir` and `--log-file`.

**IG-14 Required announcements are not log events.** The
effective-configuration line, `dev-` lever warnings and posture warnings
(clearnet-only, offline, a plaintext RPC listener bound beyond
loopback) reach the log and standard error at every start
whatever the log level. The effective-configuration line shows every
value and its source; a secret is shown as set, never its value (IG-6).

**IG-15 Home.** These rules are this file. The glossary tree and the name
tables live in `docs/design/INPUT_GRAMMAR.md`. One Rust crate holds the
table types, the value kinds and their parsers, the unit types, the
diagnostic type and the renderer; every binary builds its table from it.

## How a change is made

A new or renamed option, key, command word or keyword starts as a row in
the owning binary's table in `INPUT_GRAMMAR.md`, with every segment in
the glossary tree, and is brought to the maintainer for ruling before it
lands. A deleted feature a user could reasonably ask for again becomes a
REJECTED row; an implementation switch or a fold leaves no trace.
